A||||3||BOILERMAKER||Ignition coil driving me nuts!!||||4||
Z||000000||BOILERMAKER||08-28-2000||07:27 PM||||OK, I had an MSD ignition coil on my 2.8 and it went bad (started sparking ... could see it in the dark).  I put an OEM one back on due to the fact that it is a stock engine and it worked for a couple of days.  Now, it is back to the jitterbug down the road (slight hesitations while driving) and I know that I would see it sparking if I pulled it in the garage tonight.<P>Question, is there a way of isolating the metal "pillars" from the coil so that it doesn't spart?<P>How about this, I've heard of people changing the OEM style coil to a canister type coil.  Does anybody have a part # for this style of coil?  How did you mount it if you did change yours out?||||reg||4
Z||000001||theogre||08-28-2000||11:34 PM||||What pillars?<P>The only ways a coil will arc over are the coil is bad or the coil is dirty. If the coil is bad its bad and thats it.<P>If the arcing starts because of dirt and it's stopped early then most likely there's no permanent damage. If you leave it go it can ruin the coil.<P>Is the ignition stock other than you had an MSD coil? That can affect what other coils will work.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||08-29-2000||12:02 AM||||it can also be bad sparkplug wires and its causing the coil to spark through.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierocrazy||08-29-2000||12:26 AM||||he same problem with my MSD . I put a straight boot over the whole tower, also make sure your blower motor is running..  Electricity hates heat||||reg||1
